   203	   properties alone: **pin each of the twelve expected strings exactly**, so
   204	   an ambiguity in the cap, the ellipsis or the stripping fails the test
   205	   instead of passing two different implementations. Properties to assert
   206	   alongside them: non-empty, no `#`/`**`/`` ` ``/`[…](…)`, length ≤120
   207	   including any ellipsis, never ending mid-word. The reference run of the
   208	   derivation as specified (lengths 119, 16, 115, 119, 115, 119, 116, 119,
   209	   120, 117, 117, 68 for P-001…P-013) is in this opus's evidence; note the
   210	   120 for P-010 is the pre-land-7 implementation and must become 119+`…`
   211	   under the corrected cap rule.
   212	2. **`subject:` in front matter wins**, verbatim, including when it differs
   213	   from what the body would derive, and including when the body is empty.
   214	3. **The body reaches `/api/inbox` untruncated.** End to end against a **temp
   215	   officina**, and the fixture has to be built rather than borrowed (land 2):
   216	   `needsYou()` exposes only `state: needs_you` (`store.ts:302`), P-013 is
   217	   `resolved`, and **P-012 is the only `needs_you` petitio on disk**. So copy
   218	   P-013's five-option body into a temp officina with `state: needs_you`, or
   219	   assert against P-012. A multi-paragraph, 2+ KB body arrives byte-identical
   220	   with newlines intact. Revision 1 asserted this against the real officina,
   221	   which was impossible.
   222	4. **Empty bodies, and `subject:`'s two failure levels.** A petitio with an
   223	   empty or whitespace-only body yields `body: ""` and a **non-empty**
   224	   derived subject (fall back to the petitio id rather than an empty label),
   225	   and the client's placeholder branch still renders on `body: ""`. Then the
   226	   rule: `subject: ""`, `subject: "   "`, `subject: 42` and `subject: [a]`
   227	   each produce a **blocking** `petitio.subject` finding and are **not**
   228	   rescued by the derivation; a petitio with no `subject:` key produces an
   229	   **advisory** one; a valid non-blank string produces neither.
   230	5. **Nothing else in the snapshot moves.** `Petitio` gaining a field leaves
   231	   `opera`, `acta` and the `needsYou()` opera rows deep-equal to what they
   232	   were, and `checkStudio` reports no new finding on the real officina.
   233	
   234	Reds via `bisellium red W-076 --behaviour <n> --sella <id> --studio studio --
   235	<cmd>`, one per behaviour, assertion-level, no two sharing an output
   236	(engineering lex §2, `opus.red_evidence`). Temp officinae under `$TMPDIR`
   237	for anything that writes; behaviour 1 reads the real `studio/petitiones/`
   238	without writing.
   239	
   240	## Acceptance
   241	
   242	1. `npm test` green; `typecheck`, `lint`, `format:check` clean.
   243	2. **The twelve real subjects, printed before and after, kept as evidence.**
   244	   This opus's claim is a measurement; the diff in that output is the
   245	   acceptance. **12 of 12 must change** — revision 1 said 11, which was a
   246	   guess about P-002 and is wrong: its `#` is stripped, so it changes too.
   247	3. `GET /api/inbox` on the real officina returns **P-012**'s full body — the
   248	   only `needs_you` petitio on disk. P-013 is `resolved` and does not surface.
   249	4. The placeholder branch still renders for `body: ""`, asserted directly.
   250	   Revision 1 asserted "`inboxview.test.ts` passes unchanged", which land 4
   251	   invalidated: three fixtures gain `body: ""`.
   252	5. `bisellium check studio --repo .` reports the twelve advisory
   253	   `petitio.subject` findings and **no new blocking finding**.
   254	
   255	## House constraints
   256	
   257	TypeScript strict + `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`; ESM `.js` specifiers;
   258	Node ≥ 25; `packages/cli/src/check.ts`'s house style. No new dependency.
   259	
   260	## Out of scope
   261	
   262	- **Rendering markdown as markdown.** Needs a dependency and a decision.
   263	  `pre-wrap` preserves the author's structure, which is enough to rule on.
   264	- **Backfilling `subject:` into the twelve existing petitiones.** The
   265	  fallback makes it unnecessary, and there is no CLI writer for a petitio
   266	  until W-038.
   267	- **Subjects for opera rows.** `needsYou().opera` carries `title`, which is
   268	  already a real field with a real writer (`amend`, W-062). Not this defect.
   269	- **Inbox paging or search.** Three open petitiones.
   270	
   271	## For Sol
   272	
   273	Sol keeps the slot; not a UI opus, so D-022's `ui-lead` exception does not
   274	apply.
   275	
   276	**Revision 1's four targets are spent.** Target 1 (misleading truncation)
   277	became land 6 and changed the design — P-004 is the instance, and it inverts
   278	meaning rather than shortening it. Target 2 (stripping against real inputs)
   279	became land 7; the twelve outputs are now pinned. Target 3 (`body?`) became
   280	land 4 and the `?` is gone. Target 4 (the CSS line's authority) became land 9
   281	and the line is withdrawn. Revision 2's own surface:
   282	
   283	1. **Whether "required for new petitiones" is enforceable at all today.**
   284	   Nothing writes a petitio — W-038 is backlog — so the requirement rests on
   285	   an advisory finding plus whoever hand-writes the file. That is the
   286	   injection-by-convention this officina keeps replacing with mechanism.
   287	   Is an advisory enough until W-038, or should `petitio.subject` block on
   288	   absence and force the twelve to be backfilled now?
   289	2. **The legacy fallback's blast radius, given land 6.** I have labelled the
   290	   derivation legacy and named P-004 as a known-misleading output rather than
   291	   trying to out-clever it. Find a second one: a petitio whose derived
   292	   subject is not merely incomplete but says something the body contradicts.
   293	   If there are three, "legacy fallback" is too generous and the twelve need
   294	   real subjects.
   295	3. **The pinned twelve as a maintenance cost.** Pinning exact strings makes
   296	   the derivation unambiguous and makes every future petitio edit a test edit.
   297	   Is that the right trade, or should only P-004, P-010 and P-013 be pinned
   298	   exactly (the truncation boundary, the cap boundary, the short case) with
   299	   the rest asserted by property?
   300	4. **`body` required versus the wire.** Making it required in `api.ts` is a
   301	   client-side type claim about a server this repo also owns. Nothing
   302	   validates the actual JSON. If the server regressed to omitting `body`,
   303	   the client would read `undefined` through a required type — is a runtime
   304	   guard warranted, or is that the kind of defensive code the type exists to
   305	   avoid?
   306	
